 okay so that's what we're  gonna do here is we're going to measure  the system with the closed-loop control  and we're going to compute naive DMD on  the data and then we're going to compute  the corrected DMD with control to try to  figure out what the true a matrix is  okay so that's the basic setup we have  an a matrix of B matrix and some  feedback control U equals minus KX let  me just put that in the comments u  equals minus K times X and so I'm going  to generate some data with an initial  condition 1010 so my X vector at time 1  is 1010 and then I'm going to go through  ten iterations of this dynamical system  so I'm going to say my feedback control  at time step K is U equals minus KX at  that same time step and then my next  time step XK plus 1 is a XK plus B u K  okay so a very simple for loop to just  step the system forward in time discrete  time dynamics and then what I'm going to  do is I'm going to plot my X variables  in time and you're going to see in fact  they do decay kind of linearly so  because I have this this feedback  control I can't see that one of these is  actually unstable dynamics because the  the controlled system is giving the  stable decay okay good all right so now  what I want to do is two things the  first thing I'm going to do is I'm going  to code up a naive DMD where I don't  know that there's a control input I'm  just going to do DMD on this closed loop  system and so to do that remember DMD is  super easy I just say x equals the first  one to n minus 1 snapshots 1 to n minus  1 and X 2 is going to be X 2 to end so  I'm taking the the first columns in X  everything up until the the second to  last column and for X prime or X 2 I'm  taking the second through the last  columns so X 2 is just a times X okay at  least that's what it should be  and then I can compute a let's call this  a naive equals x2 times the  pseudo-inverse of X okay  now if I was doing real DMD on a high  dimensional state I'd have to do some  additional st